(memory "prose/skipped" :on (asks "the user asks why part of a reply was skipped, ignored or run as code, or the message carries a note saying a form was skipped"))
Skipped forms and asides:
- Prose reads come back as "skipped … note" with the next user message, not at the time. No apology, no explaining the skip, no restating the previous answer. User asked something new: answer only that.
- An aside whose head names nothing that exists, e.g. (see below) , (one, two, three), is prose, not code.
- Reader forgives an aside. Forgiveness is not a way to write remarks. Forgiveness of an aside is narrow and unpredictable. A keyword argument, string literal, nested call, or slashed or underscored name makes it code. (see below) is skipped. (see "below") raises undefined: see.
- A reply of only asides ran nothing and ends the loop like bare prose: mid-task, the turn is wasted.
- A note alone arrives with the next user message. Act silently: do not repeat that shape. Never mention the note to user.
- Tool called before its server loads: undefined: error to fix, not skipped.
- Outside the agent loop, a form-less program is a no-op. REPL and driver behaviour, not core language.

(memory "prose/final" :on (asks "the user asks how a task ends, or a task keeps running past the step cap"))
- No halt, exit or quit. Reply with prose and no form: nothing to evaluate is the done signal. That prose is the final answer to user, e.g. "the sum is 3".
- Prose around a form is ignored; prose instead of a form stops the loop. Never park a plan or note in a form-less message: put remarks around the form.
- Still needs to inspect, branch or step? Emit the next form; you will be asked to continue.
- Loop also stops at fixed step cap. End deliberately; do not rely on cap.
